\vspace{2em}
We would like to extend our heartfelt gratitude to the Editor-in-Chief and the
Associate Editor for their time and effort in handling our paper. We also
sincerely thank the four anonymous reviewers for their invaluable comments and
suggestions, which have greatly helped us improve the quality of our work.

We have completed a major revision of the manuscript and expanded its technical
explanations in the following four main areas:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*)]
  \item validated the necessity and contribution of VLM guidance by adding two
  ablations that isolate its visual and semantic components and by extending
  comparisons with fixed and adaptive intervention strategies;
  \item improved reproducibility by providing the exact VLM prompt, model and
  inference settings, visual-input selection procedure, output processing,
  update frequency, resource usage, failure handling, and relevant training
  details;
  \item strengthened the evaluation of the proposed Bellman success-value
  critic by reporting task-outcome prediction accuracy, unsafe-event detection,
  and false-negative rates under student-controlled rollouts; and
  \item expanded the real-world evaluation by increasing the number of trials,
  reporting results for all 22 objects and pooled seen/unseen confidence
  intervals, adding a
  physical baseline comparison, and clarifying the empirical scope and
  limitations of our safety and deployment claims.
\end{enumerate}

Detailed responses to each comment are provided below. Reviewer comments are
reproduced in bold italics, and each response follows the bold
\textbf{[Response:]} label. Changes in the revised manuscript are highlighted
in blue.

\clearpage
\endgroup
\reviewerheading{Reviewer \#14}

We sincerely thank the reviewer for the careful assessment of our work and for
the constructive suggestions. Below are our point-by-point responses.

\reviewcomment{whether the VLM and visual information provide a clear advantage
over simpler adaptive approaches.}

\response We have added ablations (g) and (h) to isolate the
contribution of the VLM to the adaptive intervention pipeline. We have also
expanded the discussion of Table~III in the Results section.
\begingroup
\let\responsetable\table
\let\endresponsetable\endtable
\renewenvironment{table}[1][]{\responsetable[H]}{\endresponsetable}
\responseablationinput{../../../table/ablation_studies.tex}
\endgroup

The results provide strong evidence that VLM-based intervention outperforms both LLM-based
intervention and performance-based curriculum adaptation. Semantic information
alone reduces the unsafe exploration rate, while combining visual and semantic
information enables more precise intervention, achieving the highest task
success rate and the lowest unsafe exploration rate. Although the
performance-based curriculum reduces unsafe exploration to some extent, it
sacrifices task success, suggesting that a simple rule-based method is
insufficient for guiding intervention policies in complex scenarios.

\reviewcomment{The related work on adaptive intervention should also be
expanded.}

\response We have reformulated the Related Work section to more
clearly discuss adaptive intervention methods. In particular, AIM provides an
important foundation for our work, and we have added it to the reference list.
Compared with prior methods such as HG-DAgger and ThriftyDAgger, our proposed
pipeline does not require human intervention, enabling fully autonomous
intervention during online policy distillation.

\begin{figure}[H]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.52\textwidth]{figures/reviewer14_comment2_adaptive_intervention.png}
\end{figure}

\reviewcomment{the authors should provide the VLM prompt and explain how the
images are selected and how the VLM outputs are used.}

\response The details required to reproduce our method are provided
in the supplementary material. The exact VLM prompt and representative visual
input examples are included in Supplementary Section~B.

The algorithm block gives the full prompt template used by the runtime
implementation. Braced terms denote values serialized at query time, and the
visual-sample block is repeated once for each attached image.

\input{../../../alg/appendix_vlm_prompt_example.tex}

We used OpenAI GPT-5.5 with the rollout statistics above and up to eight
high-detail RGB images encoded as JPEG data URLs. We queried the model after
2{,}000 steps and every 10{,}000 steps thereafter (10 calls over 100{,}000
steps). Archived reports from the earlier low-detail setting contain 30{,}641
prompt and 4{,}841 completion tokens (35{,}482 total).

\begingroup\footnotesize
The requested machine-readable output is a top-level JSON object with the
following schema:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "l2_threshold": <number>, "success_threshold": <number>,
  "confidence": <number>, "reason": <string>
}
\end{verbatim}
\endgroup
The two returned thresholds are absolute candidates. Each candidate is clipped
to a fixed range around its initial value and then exponentially smoothed with
the previously applied threshold. For $x\in\{\delta,Q\}$,
\[
\bar\tau_x^{(k)}=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(
\hat\tau_x^{(k)},c_x^{\min}\tau_x^{(0)},
c_x^{\max}\tau_x^{(0)}\right),\qquad
\tau_x^{(k+1)}=(1-\alpha)\tau_x^{(k)}+\alpha\bar\tau_x^{(k)}.
\]
We use $\alpha=0.1$ and $(c_x^{\min},c_x^{\max})=(0.5,1.5)$ for both
thresholds. The updated values are applied from the next control step through
the intervention gate
$\lVert a_t^S-a_t^T\rVert_2>\tau_\delta\ \lor
\hat Q_{\mathrm{succ}}(s_t,a_t^S)<\tau_Q$; lowering $\tau_\delta$ or raising
$\tau_Q$ increases teacher intervention. The \texttt{confidence} and
\texttt{reason} fields are logged for diagnostics and do not alter the update.
This adaptation is used only during simulation distillation; physical
deployment uses the frozen RGB student alone.

If a VLM request fails or its output cannot be parsed, the controller retains
the previously applied thresholds and tries again at the next scheduled update.
None of the reported trials encountered a condition that activated this
fallback.
\reviewcomment{The Bellman success-value critic is also important for
intervention, but its performance is not evaluated in detail. Some evaluation
of the critic, especially its errors for unsafe cases, would be helpful.}

\response We evaluate the resulting student and fixed-threshold Bellman critic
from ablation~(e) and report the recorded metrics in Supplementary Section~C.1.
\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_bellman_critic_evaluation.tex}

Both networks are frozen, and teacher intervention is disabled so that the
student remains in control throughout each evaluation episode. The critic runs
in parallel as a passive evaluator whose predictions do not affect the executed
actions. This setup preserves observed unsafe terminations and allows us to
evaluate whether the critic signals low predicted safe-lift success before an
unsafe event occurs.

\reviewcomment{Please also clarify the number of runs/random seeds used for the
results.}

\response We run 30 real-world evaluations for each object, distributed equally
across random seeds 40, 41, 42, 43, and 44 (six evaluations per seed).

\reviewcomment{Finally, I am not fully convinced by the use of the term
``safety-critical.'' The current safety evaluation is based on task-specific
unsafe conditions rather than formal safety guarantees. The authors should
clarify the scope of this claim.}

\response We acknowledge that the term ``safety-critical'' is too strong for an
adaptive intervention pipeline such as ours. We have therefore revised the
title to ``VLM-DexSafeDagger: VLM-Guided Intervention Policy Towards Safer
Visuomotor Skill Distillation'' and removed or qualified the term elsewhere in
the manuscript. The revised text explicitly states that our method provides
empirical risk reduction under task-specific event definitions rather than a
formal safety guarantee, and that safety violations remain possible.

\reviewcomment{However, only VLM-DexSafeDagger evaluated on the real robot does
not sufficiently prove the proposed concept.}

\response We deploy the frozen RGB student policies resulting from
ablations~(e) and~(f) on the real robot using the same object set,
initial-condition protocol, and number of trials. We report lift-success and
unsafe-event rates for the seen and unseen object sets in Main Paper
Table~IV.
\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_physical_baseline_comparison.tex}

Only the resulting student policy is deployed; the teacher, Bellman critic, VLM, and threshold-adaptation mechanism
are not active during physical evaluation.

\reviewcomment{It would be helpful to provide overall results for all 22
objects, for example by reporting results separately for seen and unseen
objects.}

\response We have doubled the number of evaluation episodes for each object and
reported the per-object and aggregate seen/unseen results for all 22 objects in
Supplementary Tables~IV and~V.
\responsecompacttableinput{../../../table/appendix_additional_experimental_results.tex}
\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_seen_unseen_summary.tex}

As
recommended, we have also extended Tables~II--IV in the main paper to report
aggregate results for each evaluation setting.

\clearpage
\reviewerheading{Reviewer \#22}

We sincerely thank the reviewer for the detailed and constructive comments.
Below are our point-by-point responses.

\reviewcomment{The term ``safety-critical'' is too strong because the framework
supplies empirical intervention rather than formal constraint satisfaction.
State explicitly that safety violations remain possible.}

\response We acknowledge that the term ``safety-critical'' is too strong for an
adaptive intervention pipeline such as ours. We have therefore revised the
title to ``VLM-DexSafeDagger: VLM-Guided Intervention Policy Towards Safer
Visuomotor Skill Distillation'' and removed or qualified the term elsewhere in
the manuscript. The revised text explicitly states that our method provides
empirical risk reduction under task-specific event definitions rather than a
formal safety guarantee, and that safety violations remain possible.

\reviewcomment{Demonstrate that the VLM is necessary by comparing against fixed
thresholds, rule-based adaptation, Bayesian optimization, curriculum schedules,
grid search, and a small learned threshold network.}

\response We have supplemented the ablation study with variants (g) and (h).
Together with variants (e) and (f), these experiments form a controlled ladder
of adaptive intervention strategies: fixed thresholds, a performance-based
curriculum, semantics-guided intervention-policy adaptation, and
visual-semantic-guided adaptive intervention. We have added a corresponding
discussion to the Results section. Table~III shows that adaptive intervention
reduces the unsafe rate relative to fixed thresholds. However, the
curriculum-based method sacrifices lift success, whereas incorporating semantic
information recovers task performance while further reducing unsafe
exploration. Overall, combining visual and semantic information outperforms the
other approaches, achieving the best balance between lift success and unsafe
rate.

\begingroup
\let\responsetable\table
\let\endresponsetable\endtable
\renewenvironment{table}[1][]{\responsetable[H]}{\endresponsetable}
\let\responsecommenttwolabel\label
\renewcommand{\label}[1]{\responsecommenttwolabel{tab:reviewer22-comment2-ablation}}
\responseablationinput{../../../table/ablation_studies.tex}
\endgroup

\reviewcomment{The Bellman critic predicts combined ``safe-lift-success,''
thereby conflating task success with safety. Separate safety risk and task value,
and report calibration, false-negative rates, and reliability diagrams.}

\response Lift success and unsafe termination measure different aspects of an
episode and are not mutually exclusive. Lift success is recorded once the
object satisfies the prescribed height-and-hold criterion, but satisfying this
criterion does not terminate the rollout. The episode continues to be monitored
for unsafe events until timeout or unsafe termination. Consequently, an episode
may achieve lift success and subsequently terminate because of an unsafe event.
The Bellman critic is therefore trained on the stricter joint safe-lift-success
target, which is positive only when the lift criterion is satisfied without an
unsafe event.

We evaluate the resulting student and fixed-threshold Bellman critic from
ablation~(e). Both networks are frozen, teacher intervention is disabled, and
the critic runs in parallel as a passive evaluator. We separately report task
success and unsafe termination outcomes, including the critic's unsafe-episode
detection and false-negative rates. The results are reported in Supplementary Section~C.1.

\begingroup
\renewcommand{\label}[1]{}
\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_bellman_critic_evaluation.tex}
\endgroup

\reviewcomment{Teacher intervention is assumed safe, yet teacher policies may
collide or fail. Quantify teacher unsafe rates and define how the framework
responds when teacher and critic disagree.}

\response Teacher takeover is not guaranteed to be safe; it provides an
empirical tendency toward safer exploration rather than a formal guarantee. We
report the teacher policies' unsafe rates in \hyperref[tab:reviewer22-comment2-ablation]{Table~III} of the main paper and,
in this revision, provide mean statistics across all tested objects. The
intervention gate follows a conservative OR rule: intervention is triggered if
either the teacher--student action disagreement exceeds its threshold or the
critic's predicted success falls below its threshold. Thus, when the two
signals disagree, the conservative outcome is to intervene. Comparing
ablations (c) and (e) shows that adding the critic-based branch substantially
reduces the unsafe exploration rate.

\reviewcomment{Provide exact VLM prompts, model version, decoding settings,
image-selection procedure, output parsing, update frequency, latency, cost, and
failure handling. Reproducibility may otherwise depend on an external
proprietary service.}

\response The details required to reproduce our method are provided in the
supplementary material. The exact VLM prompt, representative visual-input
examples, model and inference configuration, image-selection and
output-processing procedures, update frequency, token usage, and failure
handling are included in Supplementary Section~B.

The algorithm block gives the full prompt template used by the runtime
implementation. Braced terms denote values serialized at query time, and the
visual-sample block is repeated once for each attached image.

\input{../../../alg/appendix_vlm_prompt_example.tex}

We used OpenAI GPT-5.5 with the rollout statistics above and up to eight
high-detail RGB images encoded as JPEG data URLs. We queried the model after
2{,}000 steps and every 10{,}000 steps thereafter (10 calls over 100{,}000
steps). Archived reports from the earlier low-detail setting contain 30{,}641
prompt and 4{,}841 completion tokens (35{,}482 total).

\begingroup\footnotesize
The requested machine-readable output is a top-level JSON object with the
following schema:
\begin{verbatim}
{
  "l2_threshold": <number>, "success_threshold": <number>,
  "confidence": <number>, "reason": <string>
}
\end{verbatim}
\endgroup
The two returned thresholds are absolute candidates. Each candidate is clipped
to a fixed range around its initial value and then exponentially smoothed with
the previously applied threshold. For $x\in\{\delta,Q\}$,
\[
\bar\tau_x^{(k)}=\operatorname{clip}\!\left(
\hat\tau_x^{(k)},c_x^{\min}\tau_x^{(0)},
c_x^{\max}\tau_x^{(0)}\right),\qquad
\tau_x^{(k+1)}=(1-\alpha)\tau_x^{(k)}+\alpha\bar\tau_x^{(k)}.
\]
We use $\alpha=0.1$ and $(c_x^{\min},c_x^{\max})=(0.5,1.5)$ for both
thresholds. The updated values are applied from the next control step through
the intervention gate
$\lVert a_t^S-a_t^T\rVert_2>\tau_\delta\ \lor
\hat Q_{\mathrm{succ}}(s_t,a_t^S)<\tau_Q$; lowering $\tau_\delta$ or raising
$\tau_Q$ increases teacher intervention. The \texttt{confidence} and
