This adaptation is used only during simulation distillation; physical
deployment uses the frozen RGB student alone.

If a VLM request fails or its output cannot be parsed, the controller retains
the previously applied thresholds and tries again at the next scheduled update.
None of the reported trials encountered a condition that activated this
fallback.
\reviewcomment{Real-world unseen-object performance is weak: lift success falls
to 60--66.7\%, while unsafe episodes reach 40\%. Claims of sufficient deployment
robustness should be moderated.}

\response We will remove the claim that the current results establish
sufficient robustness for deployment.

\reviewcomment{Only fifteen real-world episodes per reported object produce
coarse estimates. Increase repetitions and report confidence intervals and
statistical comparisons across all 22 objects.}

\response We have doubled the number of real-world evaluation episodes from 15
to 30 per object. We have also reported the complete results for all 22 tested
objects in Supplementary Section~C.3. Supplementary Table~V reports the
pooled means and Wilson 95\% confidence intervals for the 14 seen and eight
unseen objects.

\begingroup
\let\oodresult\relax
\renewcommand{\label}[1]{}
\responsecompacttableinput{../../../table/appendix_additional_experimental_results.tex}
\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_seen_unseen_summary.tex}
\endgroup

\reviewcomment{Safety-event thresholds for harmful collision, object escape,
palm flipping, and workspace violation require physical justification. Include
contact-force measurements and severity-weighted safety metrics.}

\response We have provided the exact definitions of all unsafe events in the
termination-conditions discussion in Supplementary Section~A.2. When multiple
unsafe conditions occur simultaneously during training, the event label is
assigned according to a clear severity-based priority: harmful collision,
object escape, palm flipped, and hand too far, in descending order.

\medskip
\noindent\textbf{Termination Conditions.}
We define four unsafe conditions that serve both as termination criteria during
teacher-policy training and as unsafe-event signals during student-policy
distillation. We additionally terminate an episode when it reaches the maximum
time horizon; however, a timeout is not classified as an unsafe event. All five
termination conditions are checked at \(60\,\text{Hz}\) in the environment
frame.

\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),leftmargin=2em,itemsep=2pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item \emph{Harmful collision:} We monitor all arm--table contacts and
  trigger this termination when the palm or any fingertip is less than
  1\,cm above the tabletop, or when any arm link contacts the table. An
  arm--table contact is registered when the $\ell_1$ norm of the filtered
  contact-force vector exceeds 0.1 N.
  \item \emph{Object escape:} We define a permissible object workspace above
  the table that covers the intended manipulation region. We trigger
  object-escape termination as soon as the object center leaves this space or
  falls below the table.
  \item \emph{Palm flipped:} The palm should face downward toward the object and
  table. We trigger this termination when its downward direction differs from
  world-down by more than $90^\circ$, at which point the nominal grasp geometry
  is inverted.
  \item \emph{Hand too far:} We use the same permissible workspace as in (b)
  and trigger this termination when the palm link leaves it, indicating that
  the hand has moved too far during exploration.
  \item \emph{Timeout:} We terminate the episode when the time index reaches
  the maximum episode horizon, $t\geq T_{\max}$.
\end{enumerate}
Expanding the termination rule used in the main paper gives
\[
\mathbb I_{\rm term}(s_t,t)
=\mathbb I_{\rm coll}(s_t)\lor\mathbb I_{\rm obj}(s_t)
 \lor\mathbb I_{\rm flip}(s_t)\lor\mathbb I_{\rm hf}(s_t)
 \lor\mathbb I_{\rm timeout}.
\]
To avoid conflicts between simultaneous termination conditions, we assign the
event label using the following descending severity priority:
$\text{harmful collision}\succ\text{object escape}\succ\text{palm flipped}
\succ\text{hand too far}\succ\text{timeout}$.

This order reflects decreasing immediacy of harm and loss of control: direct
arm--table contact poses the clearest hardware risk, object escape represents
loss of containment, palm inversion and workspace departure are geometric
failures of decreasing immediate consequence, and timeout is last because it is
not classified as unsafe.
\reviewcomment{Clarify whether VLM adaptation occurs only during simulation
distillation or during physical deployment. If absent at deployment, the role of
scene-level VLM reasoning should not be overstated.}

\response The VLM is queried only during simulation-based online distillation.
During physical deployment, the RGB student acts without the VLM, privileged
teacher, Bellman critic, or threshold adaptation. We will state this distinction
in the abstract, method, experimental setup, and limitations, and will describe
the VLM as a training-time adaptation mechanism rather than a deployment-time
safety monitor.

\reviewcomment{Compare training samples, teacher queries, interventions,
computation, and final success across Vanilla DAgger, SafeDAgger, critic-based
intervention, and VLM-guided intervention using multiple random seeds.}

\response We repeat the complete distillation process for Vanilla DAgger~(b),
Vanilla SafeDAgger~(c), critic-based intervention~(e), and VLM-guided
intervention~(f) using five independent random seeds,
$\{40,41,42,43,44\}$. All variants use the same number of training samples, and
the teacher-intervention rates exhibit only minor variation across seeds. The
final lift-success and unsafe-episode rates are reported as the mean~$\pm$~standard
deviation across seeds in Supplementary Section~C.2.

\responsetableinput{../../../table/appendix_ablation_across_seeds.tex}

\vspace{1em}
We thank the Editor and the reviewers again for their thoughtful feedback. The
requested revisions will help us present the method's contribution more clearly,
support its empirical claims more rigorously, and state its limitations more
precisely.

